CONTROLLED COMPARISON: the CFGMCLK version of the SAME probe (identical counter logic,
identical readback, identical register layout; only the clock source differs) returned
CHANGING values on every read. A counter that advances under one clock source and is
frozen under another, with everything else identical, localises the fault to the clock source.

CONSISTENT WITH the parent repository's docs/fpga/clocking.md: "Ring Oscillator (DOES NOT
WORK) ... -abc9 inserts SCC breaker that destroys the combinational loop - Behavioral
assign osc = ~osc is optimized away by Yosys - LUT1 primitive instantiation also broken by
ABC9 SCC detection".

CAVEAT, recorded honestly: the frozen reading above was taken with the FIRST counter
implementation (a hand-rolled Gray counter whose reflected-Gray update was later found to be
wrong). The controlled re-test with the IDENTICAL ripple binary counter used for the CFGMCLK
measurement is built (clock_probe_ring.bit, md5 35c167a1a58015286a58b98e33fb9b9c) and was
ready to flash when the Digilent HS2 dropped off the USB bus. That confirmation is OUTSTANDING.
